15
00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:42.740
A breakthrough from researchers in China
has demonstrated a

16
00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:47.100
viable path forward, using an entirely
different material: molybdenum

17
00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:52.580
disulfide. The Promise of 2D Materials
Molybdenum disulfide is

18
00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:55.280
a compound with an elegant structure: a
single layer

19
00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:57.920
of molybdenum atoms sandwiched between two
layers of sulfur

20
00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:00.480
atoms. It is incredibly thin — just three
atoms

21
00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.500
thick. Unlike graphene (which conducts
electricity too well and

22
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:06.640
cannot be easily "switched off"),
molybdenum disulfide behaves like

23
00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:08.960
a proper semiconductor. It can be switched
on and

24
00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.140
off cleanly, but at a scale that silicon
can

25
00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:15.780
never reach. The Breakthrough: From Lab to
Processor Scientists

26
00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:18.740
have known about 2D semiconductors for
years, but creating

27
00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:20.860
a single flawless transistor in a lab is
very

28
00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:23.446
different from manufacturing thousands of
